A||||24||sqoach||I just had a thought (relating to my overheating)||||11||
Z||000000||sqoach||04-16-2001||09:42 PM||||Now this may sound really weird, or even stupid, but I'll just throw it out there.<P>Ok, the heater core is located under the passenger side of the dash, correct? And the coolant runs through the heater core. If the coolant got stirred up enough, wouldn't it cause air bubbles? My sub is directly below the heater core. And sometimes I really crank it up. Anyone catching my drift here? Is this possible???<P>------------------<BR>sqoach@yahoo.com<P>            <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sqoach2.JPG">   <BR>84 SE (project car?)<BR>85 Coupe (parts car)<BR>86 SC (daily driver)||||reg||11
Z||000001||FieroJoe||04-16-2001||10:13 PM||||That is a very interesting thought!  And yes, I bet that you sub does stir it up a lot, but unless you have a leak in your core, it's not stirring any air into it.  You'd find coolant inside the car if you had a leak there!  The air is probably getting in somewhere else, and then being stirred up.<P>I dont think that the fact that the air is getting stirred up makes any difference with your overheating.  Just my speculation though.<P>------------------<BR>AIM: Snowboarder0311<BR>87 Coupe 5sp. - big resto in progress<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||12
Z||000002||strdream||04-16-2001||10:19 PM||||There are tiny air bubbles in you blood stream too.  They are trying to get to your brain.  Maybe you should turn it down.<BR>  As for the cooling system, the air bubbles will work their way out. I don't see how resonance in the heater core will result in poor circulation in the engine or radiator.<BR>Are you having cooling trouble?<BR>discribe the conditions that bring it on and what you have tried and what has helped.  You may have clues for us.<P>Ross||||reg||1
Z||000003||Fierowrecker||04-16-2001||10:36 PM||||What you are thinking of, is called cavitation...<BR>As soon as the sound energy is removed, the bubbles colapse...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||04-16-2001||10:43 PM||||Actually,,,, Fiero heater cores commonly fail with little or no coolant getting into the car. The only way to be sure is pull off the cover and see it it's damp. Even then the core can often seap significant air  into the system while only a drop of coolant may escape.<P>You also need to check all the hoses and fittings. Especially the fill neck in back and it's cap.<P>Don't forget to check for coolant hose connections under the car. heater hoses as well as main lines.||||reg||1
Z||000005||87GTZ34||04-16-2001||10:49 PM||||Sqoach, eliminate the possibility by bypassing the heater core.  ie connect the two heater circuits from the engine together.  All that will do is make the thermostat open more quickly.  I don't think the boom in your heater is the problem but this will eliminate it.  BTW When I suggested the pressure tester last week I didn't realize you had already tried it.  If you suspect a crack and the problem is heat related, then you will need to slap it on there when it gets hot.  Of course this is dangerous because showering with 260 degree water MAY cause injury... See if you can leave the tester on while you are getting the engine hot, see what the pressure is at (shouldn't exceed 15 lbs if you have a 15 lb cap. If that is the case then your overflow should be catching it.||||reg||1
Z||000006||sqoach||04-17-2001||01:13 PM||||Well, the reason I brought this up is because my car only overheats after I've driven it a few times. Right after I refill and burp the system, it does fine. I was thinking maybe the water was getting stirred up and creating air bubbles everytime I drive with my stereo going. I guess I should try driving without having my stereo on at all for a while to make sure.||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||04-17-2001||07:06 PM||||The fact you've aparently had to fill it multiple times should tell you something.... the coolant has to go someplace. A stereo might stir it up but would not make air enter the system.||||reg||1
Z||000008||WKDFIRO||04-17-2001||09:05 PM||||You still haven't mentioned which engine it is (or I just looked past it.) If its the V6 its possible that the water pump has the plastic impellor design and is not circulating the water. The impellor cracks and will not keep up with the pully. There are no symptoms to this problem just the overheating. We would reach this conclusion at the shop after taking a preasure test of the coolant system to find any leaks.<P>Unfortunately this is a problem with the V6s only. The fours had the metal impellor on all of them.<P>Hope that helps.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Old Lar||04-17-2001||11:00 PM||||Maybe you could drive the car with the radio off and see if the car overheats.||||reg||3
Z||000010||sqoach||04-18-2001||01:03 PM||||theogre, the reason I've had to refill it is because when it did overheat, sometimes it would overflow out of the bottle. I don't really think I'm losing coolant, but I haven't been watching it closely enough.<P>WKDFIRO, mine's a 2.5<P>Well, so far so good. It's been doing just fine for a few days now. I've been driving it quite a bit, and with the stereo down, too. Of course I had the radiator rodded out and stuff, so maybe that did help. I'll keep you guys updated. Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Spektyr||04-18-2001||06:05 PM||||Someone mentioned cavitation, and being ex-Navy I just had to speak up.<P>Cavitation is NOT air bubbles.  Cavitation causes steam bubbles (gaseous water).  IF (and this is a big if) you cause a low pressure area in water of sufficient magnitude, the water will boil into steam at a much lower than normal temperature.  One the bubble travels outside of this low pressure area it collapses under the normal water pressure back into water.  This makes a rather loud noise, and is the number one way to detect fast moving submarines.  (Their screw (propeller) creates low pressure on it's trailing edge when under high power).  By rather loud, I mean it goes 'pop' loud enough that if you have a few thousand bubbles doing this behind a screw with 30,000 shaft horsepower, it's pretty hard to miss this within say, about 6 nautical miles of the submarine that's hauling ass.<P>Submarine active sonar (the one that goes "Ping" in the movies) can also flash boil water at the surface.  However, this is an ultrasonic 'stereo system' that is outputing an insane amount of wattage.  If your sub was running anywhere near this much power, it'd knock you unconcious everytime it hit.  (Active sonar can kill fish, and knock swimming people out at a pretty respectable distance.)<P>So no, it's highly unlikely that your subwoofer is causing trouble in your cooling system.<P><BR>-"How do you remember all this stuff?"||||reg||1
Z||000012||strdream||04-18-2001||06:34 PM||||Hey!  I didn't know you were generating air bubbles and not knowing what from.<BR>Someone should have mentioned head gaskets.<BR>Ross||||reg||1
Z||000013||sqoach||04-18-2001||07:01 PM||||Well, I can honestly say that my system does not knock me out when the bass hits. It was just a thought. I can't seem to locate the problem. I'm not sure if air bubbles are being made, but I think they are. And if my head gasket does have a leak, it must be very tiny. Coolant looks normal. Oil looks normal except on the cap. Looks like some water may have gotten in, but it's only on the cap (underside of it, that is)||||reg||1
Z||000014||sqoach||04-19-2001||11:58 AM||||DOH!<P>Well, it blew again! I figured it would after a while. And it happened pretty quick again.<P>But I don't think the engine is really getting hot. And here's a really good reason: I re-burped it and stuff about a half hour after it "overheated" then it ran perfectly again. The temp was already way down below 160`F. How could it have cooled down so fast if it was overheating? It couldn't ( could it??  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"> ), which means it must not be overheating.<P>Ok, here's another brilliant theory of mine   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><BR>When air hits the sensor for the temp gauge, it'll go wacky. Well, that explains why the temp guage wouldn't read correctly, but not why the overflow bottle would fill up. So, I'm guessing a lot of pressure is building up, more than normal, and it is forcing the coolant out. Maybe compression is leaking into the coolant system?? I don't know if that sounds right, but I'm just asking. <BR><p>[This message has been edited by sqoach (edited 04-19-2001).]||||reg||11
Z||000015||sqoach||04-19-2001||11:59 AM||||oops<BR><p>[This message has been edited by sqoach (edited 04-19-2001).]||||reg||11
Z||000016||84Bill||04-19-2001||12:34 PM||||Ya know a funny thing happend to me when my 88L4 started overheating for no apparent reason and the heater would start to blow cold. I was very confused when I poped off the rear cap and found the system FULL. So I went on my merry way and it began to overheat again!!! What the !!!! so again I checked the rear and sure nuff its fine then I went to the front and low and behold it took almost 2 gallons!!! what and why this happend I dont know but the radiator was nearly empty. Now she's topped off and I goo on my merry way and damn if it doesnt start overheating again! I pull over and popped the front hood and squeezed the radiator hose several times, it was tight and cool to the touch. Clogged radiator I assumed but when I got on my way again it was running nice and normal, best I can figure is that sediment (scale)was building up in the pipes and by squeezing the hose a few time loosend some of it up.<BR>I have decided to part out the ole 88 since I got this nice 84 SE, the 88 has been totaled (accidents) 3 times and I rebuilt her the last 2 times but the frame is hurting pretty bad now. I have not gotten down to the radiator and plumbing yet. I'll keep you all posted on what I find if your interested. ||||reg||1
